====================================================================================
1. EXACT PRIOR  (k uniform on [0,N))
====================================================================================
two independent digit-DPs agree elementwise : True
sum_b cnt[b] == N exactly                   : True
first b where #{k<N: pc=b} != C(256,b)      : b = 128  (gap = 1 )
=> for ALL b <= 127 the truncation effect is EXACTLY ZERO, not merely 2^-128-small
2^256 - N = 432420386565659656852420866394968145599 = 2^128.3457
total deficit (2^256-N)/2^256               = 2^-127.6543
worst per-class relative deficit in [10,246]: b=246, 2^-10.16
mode of the exact prior                     : b = 128
P(w=128) = 2^-4.3272 ; mean=128 ; sd=8 exactly (untruncated)

====================================================================================
2. POSTERIOR  |  conditioning events
====================================================================================
A  w >= 10   (X: unsigned weight <= 9 exhausted, 0 hits)  -> kills b in [0,9]
B  w <= 246  (Y: complement weight <= 9 exhausted)        -> kills b in [247,256]
   A and B are DISJOINT restrictions of the support: not nested, nothing multiplied.

P_prior(w<=9)    = 2^-202.6212
P_prior(w>=247)  = 2^-202.6239   (not equal to the above: [0,N) is not symmetric)
P_prior(excluded)= 2^-201.6226   <-- THIS IS TV(prior, posterior)
information gained by the entire campaign about w:
   -log2(1 - P_excl) = P_excl/ln2 = 2^-201.0938 bits = 2.916e-61 bits
   KL(post||prior)   = same to leading order = 2^-201.0938 bits

cross-check against agent X's per-bit figure < 2^-200.8:
